From: radeon-custom rs480 reset-recovery track
Subject: [PATCH] drm/radeon: SIGBUS userspace VRAM mmap access on a parked RS480

Fire 24b survived the quiet window (0058 replicated) and died silently
in the post-harness window: no fbdev-gate breadcrumb, no radeon line at
all. A killer that never enters a gated kernel path is a userspace one:
X teardown touching its CPU-mapped VRAM buffer objects issues the
non-posted HyperTransport read directly through the host aperture,
which a parked RS480 holds parked -- the register black hole through
memory space, invisible to every kernel print.

Two page-table-only gates complete the aperture closure that 0059
started on the kernel fbcon side:

1. radeon_gem_fault returns VM_FAULT_SIGBUS for VRAM-placed buffer
   objects under gpu_parked, before taking any lock. The faulting
   client dies; its buffer objects already outlive teardown through
   the parked GEM-leak path. GTT and system placements stay mapped --
   their pages are plain system RAM.

2. The parked failure path calls unmap_mapping_range on the GEM offset
   mapping after the async quiesce, zapping every live userspace PTE
   so the next touch re-faults into the SIGBUS gate instead of the
   bus. Breadcrumbed for netconsole classification.

--- a/radeon/radeon_gem.c
+++ b/radeon/radeon_gem.c
@@ -61,6 +61,22 @@
 	struct radeon_device *rdev = radeon_get_rdev(bo->bdev);
 	vm_fault_t ret;
 
+	/* A userspace touch of a CPU-mapped VRAM page goes through the
+	 * host aperture, and a parked RS480 holds MC aperture requests
+	 * parked: the access becomes a non-posted HyperTransport read
+	 * that hard-locks the machine from userspace, with no kernel
+	 * print possible. Kill the faulting client with SIGBUS instead;
+	 * its buffer objects already outlive teardown via the parked
+	 * GEM-leak path. GTT and system placements stay mapped -- their
+	 * pages are plain system RAM.
+	 */
+	if (rdev->gpu_parked &&
+	    bo->resource && bo->resource->mem_type == TTM_PL_VRAM) {
+		dev_err_once(rdev->dev,
+			     "parked: SIGBUS on VRAM mmap fault (aperture unreadable)\n");
+		return VM_FAULT_SIGBUS;
+	}
+
 	down_read(&rdev->pm.mclk_lock);
 
 	ret = ttm_bo_vm_reserve(bo, vmf);
--- a/radeon/radeon_device.c
+++ b/radeon/radeon_device.c
@@ -2041,6 +2041,17 @@
 		dev_err(rdev->dev, "parked: disabling KMS output poll worker (sync)\n");
 		drm_kms_helper_poll_disable(rdev_to_drm(rdev));
 		msleep(1);
+		/* Userspace holds live CPU mappings of VRAM buffer objects
+		 * through the GEM offset space; any touch of those pages is
+		 * the aperture black hole described in radeon_gem_fault. Zap
+		 * every PTE in the GEM mapping so the next touch re-faults
+		 * into the gpu_parked SIGBUS gate instead of the bus. Pure
+		 * page-table surgery, no hardware access.
+		 */
+		dev_err(rdev->dev, "parked: zapping userspace GEM mappings (SIGBUS on re-fault)\n");
+		unmap_mapping_range(rdev_to_drm(rdev)->anon_inode->i_mapping,
+				    0, 0, 1);
+		msleep(1);
 		dev_err(rdev->dev, "parked: async agents quiesced, entering quiet epoch\n");
 		rdev->in_reset = true;
 		rdev->needs_reset = false;
